\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	1.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul What are Calories and Macronutrients?\
\pard\tx216\tx432\pardeftab560\li432\fi-432\partightenfactor0
\ls49\ilvl1
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Calorie Source:
\f1\i0  Calories, the energy in food, come from carbohydrates, proteins, and fats.\
\ls49\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Macronutrient Energy:
\f1\i0  Carbohydrates and proteins provide 4 calories per gram, while fats provide 9 calories per gram.\
\ls49\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Macronutrient Examples:
\f1\i0  Carbohydrates include bread, pasta, and fruits; proteins include meat, dairy, and beans; fats include oils, nuts, and avocados.\
\ls49\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Carbohydrates:
\f1\i0  provide the most energy to the body and are the primary source of energy for the brain.\
\ls49\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Proteins
\f1\i0 : building blocks for the structure and function of cells. Protein sources are important to provide energy and rebuild muscles.\
\ls49\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Saturated fat:
\f1\i0  found mostly in animal-based foods, such as beef and full-fat dairy.\
\ls49\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Unsaturated fat: found in most plant-based foods, such as olive oil, peanut oil, avocados, and peanut butter.\
\ls49\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Trans fat:
\f1\i0  found in foods such as pizza, doughnuts, and cookies. Trans fats are typically not natural, but instead, are chemically produced to extend the shelf life of food.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Vocab\
\pard\tx20\tx196\pardeftab720\li196\fi-197\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls50\ilvl0
\f1\b0 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Dietary Guidelines for Americans (DGA): 
\f1\i0 A set of guidelines that are updated every five years to guide policymakers, nutrition experts (like Registered Dietitian Nutritionists), and other health professionals to promote health and prevent disease.\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Dietary Pattern:
\f1\i0  The combination of foods and beverages that constitutes an individual\'92s complete dietary intake over time. This may be a description of a customary way of eating or a description of a combination of foods recommended for consumption.\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Food Group Plan: 
\f1\i0 A plan to help you build a diet from clusters of foods that are similar in nutrient content.\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The Five Food Groups: 
\f1\i0 Fruits, Vegetables, Grains, Protein Foods, and Dairy.\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone United States Department of Agriculture (USDA):
\f1\i0  A department responsible for developing and executing laws related to farming, forestry, rural economic development, and food.\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR): 
\f1\i0 The rate at which the body uses energy while at rest to keep vital functions going, such as breathing and keeping warm.\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Estimated Energy Requirement (EER):
\f1\i0  The average dietary energy intake that is predicted to maintain energy balance in a healthy adult of a defined age, gender, weight, height, and a level of physical activity that\'a0is consistent with good health.\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Acceptable Macronutrient Distribution Range (AMDR): 
\f1\i0 Provides guidelines on how many macronutrient calories one should consume a day.\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Diet:
\f1\i0  Anything that is consumed by a particular person or people on a regular basis\
\ls50\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Dietary Reference Intakes or DRIs:
\f1\i0  are reference values of nutrient intake that help with nutrition planning and assessment of healthy individuals.\
\pard\tx180\tx376\pardeftab720\li376\fi-377\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls50\ilvl1\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA):
\f1\i0  the average daily dietary intake level that is sufficient to meet the nutrient requirement of nearly all (about 97%) healthy individuals in a group. This is the basic quantity of a nutrient recommended.\
\ls50\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Adequate Intake (AI): 
\f1\i0 a value based on observed or experimentally determined approximations of nutrient intake by a group (or groups) of healthy people\'97used when an RDA cannot be determined. This is the minimum amount of a nutrient needed for maintaining health.\
\ls50\ilvl1\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL):
\f1\i0  the highest level of daily nutrient intake that is likely to pose no risk of adverse health effects to almost all individuals in the general population. As intake increases above the UL, the risk of adverse effects increases. This is the maximum that would be consumed prior to developing negative effects of eating too much. This is not a level that is met but rather one that is avoided to prevent a decrease in health.\
\pard\tx180\tx376\pardeftab720\li376\fi-377\partightenfactor0
\ls50\ilvl1\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Estimated Average Requirement (EAR): 
\f1\i0 a nutrient intake value that is estimated to meet the requirement of half the healthy individuals in a group. These nutrient values should be used as goals for dietary intake for health.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
