\ls54\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Macronutrients are those nutrients needed by our bodies in large amounts.\
\ls54\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and water. \
\ls54\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone A kilocalorie (Calorie) is the amount of heat that raises the temperature of 1 kilogram of water by 1 degree Celsius.\
\pard\tx144\tx480\pardeftab720\li480\fi-480\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls55\ilvl1
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	b.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Carbohydrates\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls56\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Carbohydrates' main function is to provide energy for the body and, specifically, the brain. The brain solely relies on carbohydrates to provide it energy.\
\ls56\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Carbohydrates are made up of starches, fiber, and sugar. \
\ls56\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Carbohydrates are broadly classified into two forms based on their chemical structure: simple carbohydrates and complex carbohydrates.\
\ls56\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Simple carbohydrates:
\f1\i0  one or two basic units of glucose. \cb4 These are called mono- or di-saccharides. Examples of simple sugars include table sugar (sucrose) and glucose,\cb1  the type of sugar that circulates in your blood.\
\ls56\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Complex carbohydrates: 
\f1\i0 are long chains of simple sugars that can be unbranched or branched. These are called \cb4 polysaccharides\cb1 .\
\ls56\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The body breaks down most of the complex carbohydrates into glucose. Glucose goes into our bloodstream, is transported to our cells, stored, or for energy.\
\ls56\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Another complex carbohydrate is called fiber. What makes fiber different is that our digestive enzymes cannot break it down, so it passes through our digestive tract undigested unless the bacteria living in our colon or large intestine break it down. This is why having a healthy gut microbiome is so important, and we will learn about that more later on.\
\ls56\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone One gram of carbohydrates yields four calories of energy. Glucose can be stored for later or used immediately. \
\ls56\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone In humans, the storage molecule of carbohydrates is called \cb4 glycogen\cb1 , and in plants, it is called starch.\
\pard\tx144\tx480\pardeftab720\li480\fi-480\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls57\ilvl1
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	c.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Protein\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls58\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Proteins are made of amino acids. Amino acids are simple subunits composed of carbon, oxygen, hydrogen, and nitrogen. \
\ls58\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Protein can provide energy, but its main function is to provide structure and function to cells, muscles, bones, and skin.\
\ls58\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Consuming diets that are too high in protein could lead to health issues including metabolic issues of the liver, kidneys, and bones, and increase the risk of cardiovascular disease.\
\ls58\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Not consuming enough protein could lead to weakness, fatigue, possible anemia, and muscle loss. \
\ls58\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Proteins provide four kilocalories of energy per gram.\
\pard\tx144\tx480\pardeftab720\li480\fi-480\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls59\ilvl1
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	d.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Lipids\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls60\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Lipids (or fats) are also a family of molecules composed of carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen. \
\ls60\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Fat provides energy, protects our organs, plays a critical role within cells and hormones, and regulates our body temperature. \
\ls60\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Fat can be categorized into\cb4  triglycerides, phospholipids, and sterols.\cb1  \
\ls60\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Fats can also be separated into trans fat, saturated fat, and unsaturated fat.\
\ls60\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Excessive consumption of fats could lead to hyperlipidemia or possible obesity. \
\ls60\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone If not enough fat is consumed, this could lead to vitamin deficiencies because fat helps with the absorption of other nutrients. \
\ls60\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Lipids provide nine calories per gram.\
\pard\tx144\tx480\pardeftab720\li480\fi-480\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls61\ilvl1
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	e.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Water\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls62\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Water is a macronutrient but does not provide any energy. \
\ls62\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Water's main function is for transportation, chemical reactions, and body temperature regulation, digestion, and lubricating joints. \
\ls62\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Water is unique because humans can only go three days without water. \
\pard\tx144\tx480\pardeftab720\li480\fi-480\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls63\ilvl1
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	f.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Micronutrients\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls64\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Micronutrients are required by the body in smaller amounts but are still essential to help our bodies function well. \
\ls64\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone There are 16 minerals and 13 vitamins that are considered essential micronutrients. \
\ls64\ilvl2\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Micronutrients do not provide direct energy (calories), but they help to make energy when they act as components of enzymes (coenzymes). Enzymes are proteins that catalyze chemical reactions. Enzymes are important for almost all of our body functions and help with digestion.\
\pard\tx144\tx480\pardeftab720\li480\fi-480\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls65\ilvl1
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	g.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Minerals\
