\pard\tx20\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls93\ilvl0
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul The Digestive Tract\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls94\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The digestive system is made up of the gastrointestinal tract, the liver, pancreas, and gallbladder. \
\ls94\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The 3 is a series of organs that form a tube from the mouth to the anus. \
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls94\ilvl2\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The hollow organs that make up the GI tract are the mouth, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, and anus.\
\ls94\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The liver, pancreas, and gallbladder are the solid organs of the digestive system.\
\ls94\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Steps:\
\pard\tx720\tx936\pardeftab720\li936\fi-936\partightenfactor0
\ls94\ilvl3\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The tongue (skeletal muscle), and the smooth muscles of the 
\f0\b pharynx
\f1\b0  and 
\f0\b esophagus
\f1\b0  swallow the 
\f0\b bolus
\f1\b0  of food, which enters the esophagus.\
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Within the esophagus, muscular contraction, known as 
\f0\b peristalsis
\f1\b0 , pushes the bolus and liquid from the mouth into the stomach.\
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Once the bolus has gotten to the top of the stomach, it must first pass through the 
\f0\b lower esophageal sphincter
\f1\b0 . This is a muscle that controls food entering the stomach and exiting the stomach back into the esophagus.\
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The lower esophageal sphincter relaxes, letting the bolus into the upper portion of the stomach, and will contract again to prevent food from flowing back into the esophagus.\
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Through this process, the food begins to mix through peristalsis from the fundus, the upper portion of the stomach to the corpus, the midportion, and then to the antrum and pylorus, the lower portion of the stomach.\
\ls94\ilvl3\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The food is now mixed into \cb4 chyme\cb1 . This mixture of chyme continuously mixes and is slowly released in small quantities into the small intestine through the lower pyloric sphincter. The lower pyloric sphincter is a muscle that relaxes to slowly release small amounts of the contents of the stomach and then contracts to help prevent backflow into the stomach.\
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The contents next enter the small intestine, which is broken down into three parts, \cb4 the duodenum, the jejunum, and the ileum. \cb1 When the chyme enters the duodenum, \cb4 the pancreas, gallbladder, and duodenal epithelium secrete more enzymes that contain sodium bicarbonate. \cb1 Most of the digestion occurs within the duodenum and upper part of the jejunum.\
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Bile is released into the small intestine from the gallbladder. Bile contains bile salts that promote digestion and absorption of fats, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins.\cb1  A large number of enzymes are released that play a large part in hydrolyzing different parts of the makeup of food. The enzymes are pancreatic lipase and amylase, trypsin, chymotrypsin, carboxypeptidase, aminopeptidase, ribonuclease, and deoxyribonuclease. These enzymes do a combination of hydrolysis, forming monoglycerides, fatty acids, esters, dextrins, maltose, peptide bonds, amino acids, and mononucleotides. At this point, the ileocecal valve is a muscle that is helping control the amount of chyme that is leaving the small intestine and goes into the large intestine.\
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The chyme, which has been down into smaller particles from the enzymes in the small intestine, now entered the large intestine.  The mixture still contains complex starches and ingested fiber. \
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The large intestine is a 5- to 6-foot-long muscular tube that connects the cecum, ascending colon, transverse colon, descending colon, and sigmoid colon as it connects to the rectum. \
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The chyme enters the large intestine, and absorption, as well as bacterial fermentation, begins. Most of the fluid that was consumed is absorbed through the large intestine, and the rest is excreted out. \
\ls94\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The colon ferments the rest of the carbohydrates and amino acids and synthesizes some vitamins. Whatever contents are not reabsorbed back into the body, are later excreted out through the anal tract.\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
