\ls95\ilvl0
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul The Food Digestion Process\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls96\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone What role does the esophagus play in food digestion? After you swallow, peristalsis pushes the food down your esophagus into your stomach.\
\ls96\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Where in your body is bile produced? Your liver makes a digestive fluid called bile that helps digest fats and some vitamins. Bile ducts carry bile from your liver to your gallbladder for storage, or to the small intestine for use.\
\ls96\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone What is the function of the gallbladder? Your gallbladder stores bile between meals. When you eat, your gallbladder squeezes bile through the bile ducts into your small intestine.\
\ls96\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Where in your body does the primary breakdown of proteins, carbohydrates, and fats occur? Your small intestine makes digestive juice, which mixes with bile and pancreatic juice to complete the breakdown of proteins, carbohydrates, and fats. Bacteria in your small intestine make some of the enzymes you need to digest carbohydrates. Your small intestine moves water from your bloodstream into your GI tract to help break down food. Your small intestine also absorbs water with other nutrients.\
\ls96\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Where in your body is stool (or feces) formed? In your large intestine, more water moves from your GI tract into your bloodstream. Bacteria in your large intestine help break down remaining nutrients and make vitamin K. Waste products of digestion, including parts of food that are still too large, become stool.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa80\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Vocab\
\pard\tx20\tx196\pardeftab720\li196\fi-197\sa80\partightenfactor0
\ls97\ilvl0
\f1\b0 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Anus: The rear opening of the digestive tract.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Bile: A fluid formed by the liver and stored in the gallbladder. It is ejected into the small intestine, where it helps digest fat.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Chyme: The semifluid mass of partly digested food expelled by the stomach into the duodenum.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Digestive Tract: Organs that food and liquids travel through when they are swallowed, digested, absorbed, and leave the body as feces. It includes the mouth, pharynx (throat), esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, rectum, and anus.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Duodenum: The first part of the small intestine expanding from the pylorus to the jejunum.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Esophagus: A tube that transfers food from the mouth to the stomach. It is about nine inches long.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Gallbladder: A muscular sac that stores bile from the liver.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Gastrointestinal Tract: Organs that food and liquids travel through when they are swallowed, digested, absorbed, and leave the body as feces. These organs include the mouth, pharynx (throat), esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, rectum, and anus. It is part of the digestive system. Also called the alimentary tract and digestive tract.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Ileocecal Valve: The valve created by two folds of mucous membrane at the opening of the ileum into the large intestine.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Ileum: The last part of the small intestine extending between the jejunum and large intestine.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Jejunum: The section of the small intestine that contains the first two fifths beyond the duodenum and that is larger, thicker-walled, and more vascular and has more circular folds than the ileum.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Large Intestine: The more terminal division of the vertebrate intestine that is wider and shorter than the small intestine, typically divided into cecum, colon, and rectum.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Liver: A large, vascular, glandular organ of vertebrates that produces bile and causes important changes in many of the substances contained in the blood which passes through it. It is in the upper right portion of the abdominal cavity, below the diaphragm.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Lower Esophageal Sphincter: Bands of muscles at the top and bottom of the esophagus that open and close to let food move through.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Lower Pyloric Sphincter: A muscular valve at the bottom of the stomach\'a0that opens to allow food to pass from the stomach to the top of the small intestine.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Mouth: Opening through which food passes into the body. It is bounded externally by lips and internally by the pharynx. It encloses the tongue, gums, and teeth.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Pancreas: A large gland of vertebrates that produces digestive enzymes and the hormones insulin and glucagon.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Peristalsis: Consecutive waves of involuntary contraction passing along the walls of a hollow muscular structure (such as the esophagus or intestine) and forcing the contents onward.\
\ls97\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Small Intestine: the narrow part of the intestine that is between the stomach and colon. It consists of duodenum, jejunum, and ileum. It produces digestive enzymes, and is the primary site of the digestion of food into small molecules which are absorbed into the body.\
\pard\tx20\tx196\pardeftab720\li196\fi-197\partightenfactor0
\ls97\ilvl0\cf2 \cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Stomach: saclike expansion of the digestive tract that is located between the esophagus and duodenum. It normally consists of a simple curved sac with an outer serous covering, a strong muscular wall that contracts rhythmically, and an inner mucous membrane lining that contains gastric glands.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0
