\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Mechanical digestion makes the food smaller so that the surface area increases, and it is easier for your body to move it through the gastrointestinal (GI) tract. \
\ls98\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Mechanical digestion starts with chewing and then in the stomach, it is churned so that digestive juices can be exposed to the food and a larger surface area is present. \
\ls98\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Segmentation: 
\f1\b0 mechanical process that happens in the small intestine and is made up of small contractions that move food contents back and forth to mix them up and facilitate absorption. \
\ls98\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Chemical digestion: digestive secretions break down food into their basic building blocks. Ex: proteins are broken into amino acids and carbohydrates are broken down into simple monosaccharides and disaccharides. Chemical digestion occurs throughout the entire digestion process.\
\pard\tx20\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls99\ilvl0
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul The Organs\'92 Role in Digestion\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls100\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone 9 organs involved in digestion:mouth, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, anus, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. \
\ls100\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The liver, gallbladder, and pancreas provide most of the secretions that aid in the digestion and absorption of foods.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls101\ilvl2\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Mouth - mechanical(chewing), chemical (amylase in saliva break down carbs)\
\ls101\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Esophagus - moves food to the stomach via peristalsis.\
\ls101\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Stomach - food enters the upper portion (fungus), then into the midsection (corpus), then and the antrum and pylorus, the bottom half of the stomach. The stomach is the thickest and strongest of the muscles in the GI tract organs, used to churn food and move it into the small intestine. \
\pard\tx720\tx936\pardeftab720\li936\fi-936\partightenfactor0
\ls101\ilvl3\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The stomach secretes: hydrochloric acid, pepsinogen, gastric lipase, mucus, gastrin, and the intrinsic factor. \
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Intrinsic factor
\f1\b0  is a secretion within the stomach that is a glycoprotein that facilitates the absorption of vitamin B12. \
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Hydrochloric
\f1\b0  acid helps break down the food that enters the stomach and mixes it into the substance known as chyme. Hydrochloric acid also kills bacteria that could be consumed with food.\
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Pepsinogen
\f1\b0  (enzyme) Combines with hydrochloric acid and is converted to pepsin, the active form of pepsinogen. The pepsin degrades the protein from the food substance. \
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Gastric lipase
\f1\b0  (enzyme) processes the triglycerides, primarily the medium and short-chain fatty acids.\
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Gastrin
\f1\b0  is a hormone that stimulates the secretion of hydrochloric acid and pepsinogen. This hormone also promotes gastric motility. \
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls101\ilvl2\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Small intestine - Includes duodenum, jejunum, and ileum. \
\pard\tx720\tx936\pardeftab720\li936\fi-936\partightenfactor0
\ls101\ilvl3\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Chyme enters the duodenum first from the stomach and combines with the secretions from the pancreas, gallbladder, and epithelium.\
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Sodium bicarbonate (from pancreas) neutralizes the pH of the acidic chyme.\
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Secretions within the small intestine include cholecystokinin, secretin, gastric inhibitory peptide, and multiple enzymes. \
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Cholecystokinin and secretin are hormones that stimulate the release of enzymes. Secretin also stimulates the release of insulin, and mucus, and decreases motility. Cholecystokinin contracts the gallbladder and slows gastric emptying and increases motility. The gastric inhibitory peptide is a hormone that reduces the motility of the intestines.\
\ls101\ilvl3\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The enzymes released within the small intestine are pancreatic lipase and amylase, trypsin, chymotrypsin, carboxypeptidase, aminopeptidase, ribonuclease, and deoxyribonuclease. These enzymes do a combination of hydrolysis, forming monoglycerides, fatty acids, esters, dextrins, maltose, peptide bonds, amino acids, and mononucleotides. 
\f0\b The word ending -ase denotes an enzyme.
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls101\ilvl2\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Liver & gallbladder: Produce bile within the small intestine, which promotes the digestion and absorption of lipids, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins. Most absorption of nutrients and water occurs within the small intestine.\
\ls101\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone By the time the contents of the GI tract reach the end of the small intestine, the three energy-yielding nutrients\'97carbohydrate, fat, and protein\'97have been digested. \
\ls101\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Anything like water, salts, or fiber that remain after absorption are moved to the large intestine or colon. The contents enter into the last organ, the large intestine where fermentation occurs. The only secretion is mucus to help lubricate the intestinal lining. Fiber carries with it some fat, cholesterol, and a few minerals and retains water as is. Intestinal bacteria ferment some fibers, producing water, gas, and small fragments of fat that provide energy for the cells of the colon. The rest is excreted as waste.\

