\
\pard\tx20\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls103\ilvl0
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	1.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Introduction to Absorption\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls103\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Absorption is a process in which the proper nutrients are extracted from the broken-down food and transported to the proper organ. The small intestine is the primary organ for absorption within the digestive system.\
\ls103\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The main organ for absorption is the small intestine, with some absorption also within the stomach and large intestine. Absorption is one of the most important aspects of nutrient digestion because if the food is not broken down and properly absorbed, the body cannot use it. The absorption process helps turn these nutrients into usable aspects for the cells throughout the body.\
\ls103\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone While in the stomach, the food is broken down by enzymes, hydrochloric acid, and the process of churning. Minimal absorption occurs within the stomach, mostly just water is absorbed here.\
\pard\tx20\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls104\ilvl0
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	2.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Absorption in the Small Intestine\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls105\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The small intestine has a 10-foot length, which provides a surface area equivalent to 1.5 bowling lanes.\
\ls105\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone To remove the absorbed molecules rapidly and provide room for more to be absorbed, a rush of circulating blood continuously washes the underside of this surface, carrying the absorbed nutrients away to the liver and other parts of the body. Absorption of nutrients into intestinal cells typically occurs by simple diffusion, facilitated diffusion, or active transport.\
\ls105\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The small intestine is separated into three sections, the duodenum, the jejunum, and the ileum. The inner surface of the small intestine looks smooth but is actually wrinkled into hundreds of folds. Each fold has thousands of fingerlike projections, known as villi, each covered with microvilli. Within the spaces between villi are crypts. Crypts are glands that secrete intestinal juices into the small intestine whereas the goblet cells secrete mucus.\
\ls105\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Most of the absorption occurs within the jejunum portion of the small intestine except for iron, which is absorbed in the duodenum, and vitamin B12 and bile salts are absorbed in the ileum. \cb1 \
\ls105\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The microvilli have epithelial cells, which play a large part in the absorption. The epithelial cells transport the nutrients digested in the small intestine into the capillaries and lacteals. \
\ls105\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The lacteals are lymphatic vessels that receive the lipids and fat-soluble vitamins from the epithelial cells. The eventually transport lipids (as triglycerides) into the bloodstream via the subclavian vein.\
\ls105\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone The capillaries receive amino acids and carbohydrates. Once a nutrient has entered the bloodstream, it may be transported to any of the cells in the body.\
\pard\tx20\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls106\ilvl0
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	3.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Absorbing Nutrients\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls107\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Nutrients are absorbed and transported through either 
\f0\b active transport
\f1\b0  or 
\f0\b passive transport
\f1\b0 . \
\ls107\ilvl2\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Passive transport
\f1\b0 : are moving from an area of high concentration to a lower concentration. Passive transport does not always require any transport proteins or adenosine triphosphate 
\f0\b (simple diffusion)
\f1\b0 . \
\pard\tx720\tx936\pardeftab720\li936\fi-936\partightenfactor0
\ls107\ilvl3\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone If the passive transport utilizes a transport protein this is 
\f0\b passive diffusion
\f1\b0 , and if the transport protein assists the nutrients in crossing the 
\f0\b \cf7 brush border
\f1\b0 \cf2  membrane, this is 
\f0\b facilitated diffusion
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls107\ilvl2\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f0\b \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Active transport
\f1\b0 : nutrients are transported to an area within the body that has a concentration differing or against the nutrient's concentration. Due to the resistance of transportation because of the varying concentrations, a transporter protein and adenosine triphosphate (ATP) energy are required.\
\pard\tx720\tx936\pardeftab720\li936\fi-936\partightenfactor0
\ls107\ilvl3\cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Against their gradient means moving from low to high, opposite of passive.\
\pard\tx20\tx360\pardeftab720\li360\fi-360\sa40\partightenfactor0
\ls108\ilvl0
\f0\b\fs28 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	4.	}\kerning1\expnd1\expndtw5
\up0 \nosupersub \ul Blood and Absorption\
\pard\tx360\tx576\pardeftab720\li576\fi-576\partightenfactor0
\ls109\ilvl2
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone All body tissues get nutrients and oxygen from the blood and deposit carbon dioxide and other wastes back into the blood. The digestive system provides nutrients and the lungs exchange oxygen and carbon dioxide. The kidneys filter wastes out of the blood to be excreted in the urine.\
\pard\tx720\tx936\pardeftab720\li936\fi-936\partightenfactor0
