\ls146\ilvl0
\f1\b0 \cf2 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Celiac Disease
\f1\i0 \cb4 : An autoimmune disorder in which the ingestion of gluten (a protein found in wheat, barley, and rye) triggers an immune response that damages the small intestine's lining.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Crohn's Disease
\f1\i0 \cb4 : A chronic inflammatory condition that affects the gastrointestinal (GI) tract, causing inflammation, pain, and other digestive issues.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Diverticulitis
\f1\i0 \cb4 : A condition that occurs when one or more of the diverticula (small, bulging pouches) in the colon become inflamed or infected.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Diverticulosis
\f1\i0 \cb4 : A condition where small, bulging pouches (called diverticula) form in the walls of the colon.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD)
\f1\i0 \cb4 : A chronic digestive condition where stomach acid or, occasionally, bile irritates the lining of the esophagus.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Gluten
\f1\i0 \cb4 : A group of proteins found in wheat, barley, rye, and their derivatives. It gives dough its elasticity, helping it rise and maintain its shape, and gives foods a chewy texture.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Gut Microbiome
\f1\i0 \cb4 : A collection of microorganisms, including fungi, viruses, bacteria, and archaea found within the gut and their overall genetic information.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD)
\f1\i0 \cb4 : An umbrella term for chronic inflammatory conditions of the GI tract.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS): A common GI disorder that affects the large intestine.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Peptic Ulcers
\f1\i0 \cb4 : Open sores that develop on the lining of the stomach, duodenum or esophagus due to the erosion caused by stomach acid.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Prebiotics
\f1\i0 \cb4 : Non-digestible fibers that encourage the growth and activity of healthy bacteria in your gut.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Probiotics
\f1\i0 \cb4 : Helpful gut bacteria that change the conditions in the GI tract to make it more hospitable for the good bacteria.\
\ls146\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Synbiotic
\f1\i0 \cb4 : A combination of prebiotics and probiotics that work together synergistically in your digestive tract to help improve the microbiota environment.\
\pard\tx20\tx196\pardeftab720\li196\fi-197\partightenfactor0
\ls146\ilvl0\cf2 \cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Ulcerative Colitis (UC)
\f1\i0 \cb4 : A chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that primarily affects the colon (large intestine) and the rectum, causing long-term inflammation and sores (ulcers) in the lining of the colon.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0
