\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR): 
\f1\i0 The rate at which the body expends energy for life-sustaining activities.\
\ls171\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Bomb Calorimeter: A tool that is used to measure how many calories a food provides.\cb1 \
\ls171\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Energy Balance:
\f1\i0  The amount of calories in is equal to the amount of calories out.\
\ls171\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Resting Metabolic Rate (RMR): 
\f1\i0 The total number of calories burned when your body is completely at rest.\cb4  (Slightly higher than BMR)\cb1 \
\ls171\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Thermogenesis:
\f1\i0  The generation of heat when the body breaks down carbohydrates, fats, or protein.\
\ls171\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Anthropometric Assessments:
\f1\i0 \cb4  The height, weight, skinfold thickness, and waist circumference measurements.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA):
\f1\i0 \cb4  A method of body composition measurement that uses a low level electrical current that's sent through the body.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Hydrostatic Weighing: 
\f1\i0 \cb4 Underwater weighing. When a body is submerged in water, there's a buoyant counterforce equal to the weight of the water that's displaced.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Lean Body Mass (LBM): 
\f1\i0 \cb4 A component of body composition. It is fat free mass composed of muscle mass, body water, and bone mass.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Visceral Fat:
\f1\i0 \cb4  Fat stored around the major organs in the abdomen.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Aerobic Metabolism:
\f1\i0 \cb4  A chemical process in which oxygen is used to make energy from carbohydrates (sugars). Also called aerobic respiration, cell respiration, and oxidative metabolism.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Anaerobic Metabolism:
\f1\i0 \cb4  The release of energy by partially breaking down glucose and glycogen but without combining oxygen. This process consumes a large amount of glucose and glycogen.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Body Composition:
\f1\i0  The relative amount of fat mass to fat-free mass.\
\ls171\ilvl0\up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Cardiorespiratory Endurance:
\f1\i0  he ability to carry out prolonged, large muscle, dynamic movements at a moderate to high level of intensity. This relates to your heart\'92s ability to pump blood and your lungs\'92 ability to take in oxygen.\cb4 \
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Creatine Phosphate:
\f1\i0 \cb4  A stored source of energy that is used quickly, that can be converted to adenosine diphosphate (ADP) and then to ATP.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Exercise:
\f1\i0  planned, structured, and repetitive movement pattern intended for fitness.\cb4 \
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone FITT:
\f1\i0 \cb4  An acronym that stands for frequency, intensity, time and type. In exercise the amount of stress on the body can be controlled by these four variables.\
\ls171\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}
\f2\i \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Flexibility:
\f1\i0  the ability to move your joints through a full range of motion.\
