\ls245\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Amylose: Unbranched chains of glucose monomers connected by \uc0\u945  1,4 glycosidic linkages.\
\ls245\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Cellulose: The most abundant natural biopolymer.\
\ls245\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Disaccharides: When two monosaccharides undergo a dehydration reaction. This is also known as a condensation reaction or dehydration synthesis.\
\ls245\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Glycogen: The storage form of glucose in humans and other vertebrates. It is made up of monomers of glucose.\
\ls245\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Ketone Group: The functional group with the structure RC(=O)R'.\
\ls245\ilvl0\cb1 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb4 \up0 \nosupersub \ulnone Monosaccharides: Simple sugars, the most common of which is glucose.\
